{"id":"d9d6efe2-604f-4eb0-a1d6-f7ba3167179d","object":"chat.completion","created":1793333229,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Read the remainder of logger.py and the course tests to confirm the expected API.\n\n```python\nr = read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 110, 200)\nprint(r[\"content\"])\nprint(\"=====\")\nprint(read_file(\".course/test_logger.py\")[\"content\"][:6000])\n```","reasoning_content":"I have all the required source info. Note that phase=edit, baseline done. I need to read remaining parts of logger.py format function (already got full formatter class and _setup_logger and init_logger). Need to check the rest of logger.py? Not necessary for edit of formatter.\n\nNow make edits.\n\n1. environ.py: add descriptor factory `int_or_none_min`? The requirement: property DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS; None when unset; int parse >=32; empty string, unparsable, or <32 raise ValueError containing variable name. Descriptor wraps exception with name already (\"{self.name}: {e}\"). But ValueError message from init includes e.g. invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''. Wrapped as \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: ...\". Good. But careful: the descriptor's __get__ catch converts to type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\") — for ValueError fine.\n\nAdd factory method in EnvVar e.g.\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int = 32) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r}, must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nEmpty string -> int(\"\") raises ValueError -> wrapped with name. Good.\n\nAdd in envs Logging section:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n```\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only max_message_chars.\n\nDefault None; otherwise must be int (not bool) and >=32 else ValueError.\n\nMarker = \"...<omitted>...\" length 15? \"...<omitted>...\" -> 3+9+3 = 15? \"<omitted>\" is 9 chars, plus 6 dots = 15. Budget 32 - 15 = 17 remaining; head = ceil(17/2) = 9, tail = 8. Matches example.\n\nImplement truncation after formatting args: The message is record.getMessage() essentially. We must truncate only the message part, not prefix, exception. Approach: compute message = record.getMessage() (this does %s substitution without touching shared record). Then in format, we need to build formatted string without exceeding. Cleanest: temporarily... no, cannot modify shared record. Instead, construct message separately:\n\nOption: in format, if budget set and message length > budget:\n- Compute truncated message.\n- Then produce output by formatting with a copy of record? But copying record with truncated msg is safe (not modifying original). Use copy.copy(record) then set msg=truncated, args=(). That preserves exc_info, stack_info, level, etc. Formatting the copy yields prefix + message + exception text. But careful: after super().format(copy), record.message attribute on copy. For multiline alignment original code uses record.message from super() format of copy. Fine.\n\nHowever tests may check that original record's msg/args unchanged — we don't modify them. But note super().format(record) sets record.message attribute — that's standard.\n\nBut the tests likely test formatter.format(record) output. Let's implement:\n\n```python\n    _OMIT_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(...)\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n        ...\n```\n\nWait, signature currently positional 4 params. Add keyword-only after use_color: `*, max_message_chars: int | None = None`. But tests call NewLineFormatter(fmt, datefmt, high_precision, use_color) positionally maybe. Keyword-only is fine.\n\nformat:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n        if max_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > max_chars:\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = self._shorten(message, max_chars)\n                record.args = ()\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\n\nCareful with record.message used in multiline prefix: after super().format(record), record.message set. For copied record, that's copy's attribute — fine.\n\nBut wait: if we pass the copy, exceptions still formatted from copy's exc_info. Fine.\n\nAlso `record.message` check in the multiline logic: `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:` — record refers to copy here, whose .message equals truncated message. The prefix computed as msg[:msg.find(record.message)] — the message appears in msg. Should be fine.\n\nEdge: what if message text also appears in prefix? Existing behaviour.\n\n_shorten:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def _shorten(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n        marker = \"...<omitted>...\"\n        remaining = budget - len(marker)\n        if remaining <= 0:\n            return message[:budget]\n        head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n        tail = remaining - head\n        return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nHmm budget >= 32 so remaining = 17 > 0.\n\nDefault setup: fmt uses envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Import envs in logger.py: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Check for circular imports? environ doesn't import logger. stats_logger imports init_logger. Fine. But logger.py currently imports no deepinfer module. Let's check if there's import cycle: deepinfer/environ imports only stdlib. Fine.\n\nBut maybe import path in package used as `from deepinfer.environ import envs` works. Tests likely import deepinfer.logger. Ok.\n\n_setup_logger: `_default_handler.setFormatter(fmt)` where fmt = NewLineFormatter(..., max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS). Note attribute access triggers get; if env var invalid, ValueError raised at setup — tests may check that? Possibly. Fine.\n\nRe-init: _setup_logger is idempotent for handler. Tests reinitialize by calling _setup_logger after rearm.\n\n3. stats_logger.py: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nNow check tests: test_real_default_handler_and_statistics_queue, test_independent_formatter_does_not_inherit_environment. Let's peek at .course tests to ensure exact expectations? They are readable maybe. Reading tests is allowed? \"never tests\" for edit. Reading may be fine but the reading check counts? Let's just read test files to be safe — allowed to read? The instruction says edit only allowed files, never tests. Reading is okay but may consume budget. I'll read test_logger.py quickly to confirm API expectations.\n\nActually let me first check remaining logger.py beyond line 110 to see if anything else relevant (e.g. another formatter usage). Read lines 110-200.\n\nAlso, potential issue: `record.args` replaced with () and msg set to string containing '%' — since args empty, no formatting. Fine.\n\nLet me plan edits and read tests quickly.\n\nLet's do combined: read logger.py 110-end, and read test files list.\n\nBudget fine.\n\nThen apply edits.\n\nActually one more consideration: The formatter truncation should apply with budget computed on message after %s substitution — record.getMessage() does that. Good.\n\nAlso `keep_full_message` attribute exactly True: use `is True`.\n\nLet's proceed."},"